## Implemented

### Feature-scoped OAuth

The client no longer requests the old broad transition grant for new sessions.

The OAuth boundary now separates:

- posting, likes, and reposts;
- profile editing;
- social-graph mutations;
- authenticated AppView reads;
- chat;
- Spaces;
- media;
- notifications.

Existing transition grants are still recognized for compatibility with already-authorized sessions, but they are not silently requested for unrelated new capabilities. Reauthorization [REDACTED] only the missing feature permissions and preserves the permissions already granted.

The implementation is in:

- [oauth-scopes.ts](/var/home/tcs/Code/atproto/upstream/social-app/src/state/session/oauth-scopes.ts)
- [oauth-session.ts](/var/home/tcs/Code/atproto/upstream/social-app/src/state/session/oauth-session.ts)
- [public OAuth metadata](/var/home/tcs/Code/atproto/upstream/social-app/public/oauth-client-metadata.json)

This follows the current AT Protocol OAuth model: authorization-code flow, PAR, PKCE S256, DPoP, issuer and DID binding, exact redirects, and scoped `repo:`, `rpc:`, `blob:`, `account:`, and `identity:` permissions. See the [AT Protocol OAuth specification](https://atproto.com/specs/oauth), [permission specification](https://atproto.com/specs/permission), and [scope builder guide](https://atproto.com/guides/scope-builder).

### Provider composition

The client now has an attributable provider-composition boundary for:

- identity resolution;
- profiles;
- threads;
- feeds;
- search;
- notifications;
- labels;
- media;
- communities.

Each observation retains:

- provider identity;
- endpoint;
- operator declaration, if supplied;
- retrieval time;
- stale/unavailable/invalid status;
- verification status;
- the returned value or error;
- disagreement information.

Reconciliation is user-policy controlled through:

- require agreement;
- first verified result;
- explicit preferred provider;
- explicit merge with a caller-supplied merge function.

A bundled provider does not receive constitutional authority merely because it ships with the client. Public providers are queried without session credentials by default, and authenticated provider use requires an explicit client factory.

